CLOSURE_CHECKS_CLASSIFIER_AUDIT: {
  "schema_version": 1,
  "migration_run_id": "498a2d27-37d7-49ee-9cf9-4d877f633f1c",
  "snapshot_timestamp": "2026-05-21T17:06:33Z",
  "ticket_id": "f691-681e-0db9-4260",
  "items": [
    {
      "original_text": "SC1 \u2014 Per-sub-PR LLM review + provenance-narrowed integration review + re-keyed cycle ledger and DefenseStore. Every per-story PR (sprint) and per-batch PR (debug-everything) triggers an internal LLM review via a new .github/workflows/review-sub-pr.yml workflow. The branches: filter targets the PR's BASE branch matching session/**, session-**, session_**, bug-batch/** (story PRs are pushed FROM story/... TO session branch; batch PRs are pushed FROM bug-batch/... to session or main \u2014 base-ref filter is the correct primitive). The cycle ledger AND DefenseStore are both keyed by (pr_number, commit_sha); migration contract specifies v1.1.0-to-v1.2.0 sentinel-pr_number rule for existing entries (old commit_sha-only entries match against any pr_number until the next review cycle re-keys them); a contract test reads a v1.1.0 entry and asserts no data loss in v1.2.0 emission. Integration review at session-to-main (ci.yml:llm-review) reuses the existing verify-session-provenance.sh logic: exit 0 \u2192 skip LLM dispatch with explicit covered-by-sub-PR-reviews liveness assertion (check-run conclusion skipped + summary message listing covered sub-PR numbers and SHAs); exit 1 (unprovenanced) or exit 2 (budget-exhausted) \u2192 keep current full-diff path. A contract test ships with this epic locking the verifier's 0/1/2 exit-code semantics. Verification: the epic's own sprint demonstrates per-story sub-PR review firing AND a synthetic test exercises a 6000+ line all-provenanced session-to-main PR that completes without LLM dispatch.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes durable system capabilities (LLM review workflow, cycle ledger keying, provenance-narrowed integration) that persist after implementation, despite heavy technical specification language"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "SC2 \u2014 Runner gracefully degrades on oversized PRs via pre-filter + file-level chunking + LLM aggregation pass + visibility trailer. Pre-filter uses .gitattributes linguist tags (linguist-generated, linguist-vendored, linguist-documentation) PLUS additive configurable ignore.glob / ignore.regex overrides. ignore.glob ships with a non-empty default value (lockfile patterns at minimum: **/package-lock.json, **/yarn.lock, **/poetry.lock, **/go.sum); both max_files and max_calls validate non-zero at config load with a warning on zero. File-level chunking EXTENDS the existing per-directory region-split (Strategy E in region_split.py) with finer-grain per-file dispatch for oversized single-file or single-directory clusters, preserving the file-atomicity invariant (single file is NEVER split across reviewers). An LLM aggregation pass synthesizes cross-file findings before posting. The aggregated PR comment includes a visibility trailer listing every file reviewed and every file skipped with skip reason. A hard upper bound max_files \u00d7 max_calls routes to admin/FP-recovery via a distinct OVER_BOUND status (NOT FP-suspected) \u2014 /dso:fp-recovery rejects PRs with OVER_BOUND status, preserving FP-recovery's signal integrity. Verification: synthetic 7000+ line direct PR with mixed reviewable/ignored files produces aggregated findings with visibility trailer; the LLM never receives the full diff; a 200-file synthetic PR routes to admin/FP-recovery without silent passing.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes durable system capabilities and behaviors (graceful degradation, chunking strategy, aggregation, visibility) that persist after implementation, despite dense specification language mixing design details with outcome"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "SC3 \u2014 Every CI gate on path to main fires per (PR, SHA); cross-workflow check naming is consistent. All workflows currently gated base_ref=='main' or pull_request: branches: [main] are audited; their pull_request branch filters are EXTENDED (additive, not narrowed) to cover session/**, session-**, session_**, bug-batch/** base refs OR an always-runs umbrella job is added so the required-check name remains stable across branches that may skip the conditional inner job. Behavioral test test-no-duplicate-checks-per-sha.sh queries the GitHub Actions runs API, groups workflow_run entries by (head_sha, check_suite_id), selects the entry with the highest run_attempt, and asserts uniqueness of check-context names across those latest-attempt entries (re-runs after failure are out-of-scope). Verification: behavioral test passes against a fixture run of the epic's own sprint; RED state on a duplicate-check fixture fails as expected.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes durable system properties (CI gates fire consistently, checks are named consistently, duplicate checks are prevented) maintained by specific mechanisms and verified by ongoing tests"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "SC4 \u2014 Merge pipeline correctness \u2014 RED tests blocked + version-bump in PR + worktree cleanup safety with 3 guards. (a) A required CI check scans .test-index for unresolved RED markers and blocks session-to-main merge if any are present; named under an umbrella job for stable branch-protection. (b) merge-to-main-pr.sh commits the version-bump on the source branch's HEAD before merge; the S5 story includes the explicit design decision (bump-before-final-review vs. bump-after-final-review-accepting-re-review-dismissal) documented in the ticket. (c) claude-safe (in agent-batch-lifecycle.sh) and harvest-worktree.sh refuse to remove a worktree when ANY of: (i) the branch has an open PR with CI in flight; (ii) the worktree has uncommitted or unpushed changes; (iii) the branch is in a protected list (main, master, develop, release/*). --force bypass prints which guard fired and the PR URL when applicable. The open-PR check uses a 3-tier fallback chain: (1) gh pr list --head <branch>, (2) gh api repos/.../commits/<sha>/pulls, (3) for fork PRs missed by both, gh pr list --search 'head:<branch>' or equivalent. S1's spike validates the tier-2 reliability for pre-merge session-branch SHAs; if it fails, tier-3 is promoted. Verification: (a) attempted RED-marker push to main is blocked; (b) sub-PR commit history shows version-bump on source branch; (c) cleanup-against-open-PR test refuses without --force and names which guard fired.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes durable system behaviors (CI blocking, version-bump commits, worktree safety guards) that persist after implementation, though the ticket's implementation-heavy language creates minor ambiguity"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "SC5 \u2014 Epic execution discipline \u2014 verifiable from commit history. (a) The first story (S1) is a hard blocker for S2-S7 AND its Definition of Done requires: (i) a manual dso:code-reviewer-standard (opus) review of S1's own sub-PR with reviewer-findings.json attached and zero unresolved critical/important findings; (ii) two spike outcomes documented in the S1 ticket comment \u2014 the branches: filter base-ref semantics confirmed by a minimal test workflow, AND the SHA-based PR lookup reliability for non-default-branch commits confirmed (or the 3rd-tier fallback promoted). (b) All stories use /dso:sprint orchestration only \u2014 no /dso:fix-bug invocations. (c) Modifications are additive in the behavior-preservation sense: existing CI/review workflows and scripts may NOT be deleted; pull_request branch filters MAY be extended to additional base refs (broadening, not narrowing); existing inline gating may have provenance-aware skip paths added INSIDE (preserving original main-base coverage). Verification: epic-close audit confirms all three sub-conditions with an explicit files-touched + behavior-preservation-rule-applied checklist attached to the epic-close comment.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "uncertain",
        "ranking": 2,
        "rationale": "mixes durable end-state properties (execution discipline practices, behavior preservation rules) with transitional verification tasks (epic audit completion, spike documentation), making it ambiguous whether this describes ongoing system capability or one-time milestone achievement"
      },
      "user_decision": "accept",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    }
  ]
}
